window.WIKI_ENTRY={"id": "concept-104", "name_zh": "或然的自己", "name_en": "probable selves", "tagline": "你没选的那些路，都有另一个你真的在走：或然的自己不是假如，是同样真实、同样有自由意志的你", "explanation": "这一页讲的不是或然实相怎么运作，而是谁在活它们。赛斯的主张很直接：你每一个念头、每一次取舍都不会凭空消失——你实现了一条，其余那些就交给你的其他部分去实现，他们在别的实相里照样是活生生的人，有完全的自主性，也照样认为自己是真正的你。书里的出版译本把这批写作“可能的自己”，英文 probable selves，和这一页的“或然的自己”是同一件事。他之所以反复强调这不是打比方，是因为最容易听歪的地方就在这儿：把它当成“想想而已”。他也留了日常能对上的线索：你以为是自己灵光一现的东西，常常是另一个自己想过没实现的思想；梦有时是他们在给你递话；你偶尔在旧照片前那种说不清的熟悉感，也在这一串里。反过来，你能借到他们的才能，他们也能接到你没用的概念，所以你手上的库藏比你以为的大。最难消化也最解放人的一点是：遗憾和罪恶感在这个图景里失去立足处，因为没有哪条路是被真正放弃的；而你自己也不是一个封闭的成品——赛斯明说，你只用那些你相信自己拥有的心理特性，所以人格根本没法定义成“就这样”。至于或然实相整体的机制，那是可能性那一页的总账；一件事怎样分出几条支线，在或然事件那一页；这一页只回答一个问题：那些人生，是哪个你在活。", "definitions": [{"zh": "当我谈到可能的自己时，当然我说的并不是人格结构的某些象征部分或用可能性这个概念来作为一个比喻。", "en": "When I speak of probable selves, of course I am not speaking of some symbolic portion of the personality structure, or using the idea of probabilities as an analogy.", "source": "Session 680"}, {"zh": "在你的范畴之外，你有一个更大的身份，但它的一部分是在你的范畴之内的，那就是你。你的“你性”（youness）就是你的“意义所在”——一个觉察焦点，意识到它自己，并且以它自己独特的癖好选择并且看待经验。", "en": "You have a greater identity outside of your context, yet a part of it is inside your context, as you. Your youness is your significance, a focus of awareness, conscious of itself, that seeks out and views experience with its own unique propensities.", "source": "Session 682"}, {"zh": "你一直以一个可能的自己存在，虽然你没有聚焦在你自己经验的知识上。", "en": "You have always existed as a probable self, though you were not focused in the knowledge of your own experience.", "source": "Session 730"}], "blocks": [{"id": "B01", "title": "你没走的那条路，有另一个你真的在走", "note": "这一页的主张先从赛斯那句最硬的话起：任何一个已经实现的行为，背后都带出无量件没实现的行为，而这些没实现的终究也会实现。他说你长出可能的自己就像一朵花长出花瓣，可每一个都会在自己的实相里走到底，把与生俱来的那些幅度经验到最完全；用你们的话说，你们挑出一个出生和一个死亡。他给“你没选”这件事一个非常具体的机制：自我会像跳棋一样跳过那些它不想实现的事件，不让那种经验进入它的自己感，而你更大身份的其他部分，恰恰接住被你否定的同一批事件，形成它们自己的自己感。但赛斯同时堵住最容易被听歪的地方——那个选择了你所否定的行动的可能的自己，在某些重要地方跟你所知的自己很不相同；你可以为自己的目的改可能性，却改不了已经走上自己路的那些可能的自己的路线，你消灭不了它，只能在你这一份里继续挑。", "src": "第 565、682、695 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 565 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 682 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 695 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-104-e0021", "zh": "如果有个别的“可能的自己”，那么自然也就有“可能的地球”，全都在走那些你没有采用的路子。在清醒状态里，以一个想象的行为来开始，有时你可以沿着“没走的路”走上一小段。", "en": "If there are individual probable selves, then of course there are probable earths, all taking roads that you have not adopted. Beginning with an act of imagination in the waking state, you can sometimes follow for a short way into the “road not taken.”", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第565节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "565", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0022", "zh": "不过这样一个实验不会把你带得太远，那个选择了你所否定的行动的“可能的自己”与你所知的自己，在某些重要的地方是很不相同的。每一件精神性的行为都打开了一个确实的新次元。就某一种方式来说，你最微不足道的念头都孕生了新的世界。", "en": "Such an experiment will not carry you too far, however, and the probable self who has chosen the action that you denied, is in important respects quite different from the self that you know. Each mental act opens up a new dimension of actuality. In a manner of speaking, your slightest thought gives birth to worlds.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第565节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "565", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0052", "zh": "这种无尽的创造力看起来可以是如此的令你目眩神移，以至于个人会像是失落在其中，但意识在所有的层面形成它自己的组织及心灵上的互动。任何意识都自动的试图在所有可能的方向表达它自己，而且的确也这么做了。在如此做时，它会经由它自己的存在体验到“一切万有”，这当然是经过它自己那熟悉的实相诠释过的。你长出可能的自己就像一朵花长出花瓣一样。不过，每个可能的自己将会在它自己的实相里走到底——那就是说，它会去经验它天生具有的那些幅度到它最完全的地步。以你们的话来说，你们挑选出一个出生及一个死亡。", "en": "Such endless creativity can seem so dazzling that the individual would appear lost within it,2 yet consciousness forms its own organizations and psychic interactions at all levels. Any consciousness automatically tries to express itself in all probable directions, and does so. In so doing it will experience All That Is through its own being, though interpreted, of course, through that familiar reality of its own. You grow probable selves as a flower grows petals. Each probable self, however, will follow through in its own reality — that is, it will experience to the fullest those dimensions inherent to it. You pick and choose one birth and one death, in your terms.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第681节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "681", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0054", "zh": "在你的范畴之外，你有一个更大的身份，但它的一部分是在你的范畴之内的，那就是你。你的“你性”（youness）就是你的“意义所在”——一个觉察焦点，意识到它自己，并且以它自己独特的癖好选择并且看待经验。可能的实相与可能的自己之存在绝没有在任何方面否定你自己的经验或个人性之价值。它们稳稳的在那儿，从确定性之不可预测的范围里，那些适合它自己特殊本性的东西。", "en": "You have a greater identity outside of your context, yet a part of it is inside your context, as you. Your youness is your significance, a focus of awareness, conscious of itself, that seeks out and views experience with its own unique propensities. The existence of probable realities and probable selves in no way denies the validity of your own experience or individuality. That rides secure, choosing from unpredictable fields of actuality those that suit its own particular nature. (With gestures, emphatically) That selfhood jumps in leapfrog fashion over events that it does not want to actualize (pause), and does not admit such experience into its selfhood. Other portions of your greater identity, however, do accept those same events rejected by you, and form their own selfhoods.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第682节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "682", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0059", "zh": "你已经摸不到在照片里的那个人了，你是的那个你，在你的经验里可以做任何你想要的改变，你可以为你自己的目的改变可能性，但你却无法改变已走上自己的路的其他可能自己之路线。所有的可能自己是相连的，他们每个彼此影响，有一个自然的相互作用，但却没有强迫。每个可能的自己有他自己的自由意志及独特性，你可以改变在你所知的可能性里你自己的经验——那经验本身就骑在无穷尽的其他可能性上。你可以把任何数目的可能事件带进你自己的经验里，但你却无法改变你实相的另一个部分之可能经验，那是说，你无法消灭它。", "en": "The person within the photograph is beyond your reach. The you that you are can make any changes you want to in your experience: You can change probabilities for your own purposes, but you cannot change the courses of other probable selves that have gone their own ways. All probable selves are connected. They each influence one another. There is a natural interaction, but no coercion. Each probable self has its own free will and uniqueness. You can change your own experience in the probability you know — which itself rides upon infinite other probabilities. You can bring into your own experience any number of probable events, but you cannot deny the probable experience of another portion of your reality. That is, you cannot annihilate it.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第695节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "695", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B02", "title": "这不是比喻：他们跟你一样真实", "note": "赛斯怕自己讲成一套心理学象征，所以先钉死：他谈可能的自己，说的不是人格结构里某个象征的部分，也不是拿可能性当一个比喻。他们也没有被放进某个超我里迷失、埋葬或否定，照样有完全的自由意志、自主性和个人性；本体就是他们，不管在这个实相还是在你不知道的其他实相里，都有充分的自由去表达一切可能的行动与发展。你之所以觉得跟他们失联，他的解释很不客气：你这幅织锦般的存在本来就不是三次元的智力能看见的，他们是你本体或灵魂的一部分，断了线只因为你贯注在具体事件上、把它当成判断实相的准绳。他还补了一句常被忽略的话：可能对你发生的那无穷无尽的事件全都发生了，只是你注意力的长度不包括那种活动；那些世界在感官资料上并不相遇，以更深的说法来说，它们是重合的。", "src": "第 565、566、680、681、730 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 565 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 566 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 680 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 681 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 730 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-104-e0020", "zh": "你自己这仿佛是幅织锦绣画的存在，根本非三次元的智力所能看到的。可是，这些可能的自己却是你的本体或灵魂的一部分，如果你与它们失去了联系，那只是因为你贯注于具体事件上而将之当作判断实相的准绳。", "en": "The tapestry of your own existence is simply such that the three-dimensional intellect cannot behold it. These probable selves, however, are a portion of your identity or soul, and if you are out of contact with them it is only because you focus upon physical events and accept them as the criteria for reality.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第565节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "565", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0029", "zh": "这些“可能的自己”并没有在某个超我中“迷失”、埋葬或被否定，没有了自由意志、自主性或个人性。反之，本体就是他们，有完全的自由去表达所有的可能行动与发展，不但在这实相里，也在其他你所不知的实相里。", "en": "They are not “lost”, buried or negated in some superself, without free will, self-determination, or individuality. Instead the identity is what they are, with full freedom to express all probable actions and developments, both in this reality and in others that you do not know.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第566节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "566", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0047", "zh": "当我谈到可能的自己时，当然我说的并不是人格结构的某些象征部分或用可能性这个概念来作为一个比喻。", "en": "Now: When I speak of probable selves, of course I am not speaking of some symbolic portion of the personality structure, or using the idea of probabilities as an analogy.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第680节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "680", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0051", "zh": "以同样的方式，你执着于一个个人生物上的历史，你也只执着于一个整体的地球历史。所有其他的一直在你周围继续着，而其他你自己的可能自己们经验着与你历史平行的他们的“历史”，就感官资料的实际说法而言，那些世界并不相遇，然而，以更深的说法来说，它们是重合的。可能对你及鲁柏发生的任何无穷无尽事件之任何一个都发生了。只不过你们注意力的长度根本不包括此种活动罢了。", "en": "In the same way that you latch upon one personal biological history, you latch upon but one mass earth history. Others go on about you all the time, and other probable selves of your own experience their “histories” parallel to yours. In practical terms of sense data, those worlds do not meet. In deeper terms they coincide. Any of the infinite number of events that could have happened to you and Ruburt [do] happen. Your attention span simply does not include such activity.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第681节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "681", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0068", "zh": "这并不意味着如你所知的你的人格常常被陷在一个子宫里，注定死在那儿，或一个假设的全我不会被生下来，而是指聚焦在肉体上之你心灵的考古历史携带着那些经验。自己并不……（停顿，眼睛闭着）……请等我们一会儿：我在找一个好的比喻，自己并不像是一个出自陶匠窑里的一个黏土人，因此，你可以说：“啊！这里是一个自己，在上面不能再添加什么了”你一直以一个可能的自己存在，虽然你没有聚焦在你自己经验的知识上。", "en": "(Intently at 9:36:) Now this does not mean that your personality as you know it was often trapped within a womb, destined to die there, or that a hypothetical whole self would not be born. It means that the archaeology of your psyche as it is physically focused carries those experiences. The self is not … (pause, eyes closed) … give us a moment; I am searching for a good analogy … the self is not like a clay figure coming from a potter’s oven, so that you can say: “Ah, here is a self, and nothing can be added to it.” You have always existed as a probable self, though you were not focused in the knowledge of your own experience.", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第730节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "730", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B03", "title": "每一个都认为自己才是真正的你", "note": "赛斯在这个概念里埋了一个视角翻转：每一个可能的自己都认为自己才是真正的你，而对他们任何一个来说，你才是那个可能的自己；只是透过内在感官，你们全都知觉到自己是这整个完形的一部分。他先说你现在根本还谈不上认同他们，可一旦遇见，你会立刻知道那是你自己的未知部分。认得出，是因为自己基本没有限制、所有部分都相连，所以可能的自己在无意识层面本来就觉察到彼此的关系。他还说每一个可能的自己也有他们未来的自己，这一串是一个多维度的身份、一种心理结构，里面装着你知道的自己、可能的自己、转世的自己，以及比你认识的自己更高度发展的许多自己。最贴心的是他拿年纪讲的那段：你还是小孩时，身份感并不包括年老；你老了，也不把自己认同成一个孩子——你进出于可能的自己，与此同时通常毫不费力地维持住一个自己的身份。", "src": "第 309、565、680、834 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 309 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 565 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 680 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 834 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-104-e0002", "zh": "所有的行动都会被表达出来。此外，现在你某种程度上具有一个根源本质，你会有很多梦是类似的。你根本还谈不上认同这些可能的自己，但是，如果你遇见它们，你立刻就会知道你已经找到你自己的未知部分。", "en": "All action will be expressed. Now to some degree you have a root nature, then, and many of your dreams will be similar. You are far from identical with these probable selves, and yet if you met them you would instantly know that you had found unknown portions of yourself.", "source": "早期课第7册 · 第309节", "year": "1968", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第7册", "session": "309", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0003", "zh": "每一个可能的自己，你看，也都有未来的自己。这个多重维度的本体是一个我们在讨论中将会谈到的一个心理结构。这个字眼包含全我，因为它是由你知道的自己、可能的自己、转世的自己，以及比你认识的自己更加高度发展的许多自己组成的。", "en": "Each probable self you see also has future selves. This multidimensional identity is a psychological structure with which we shall be dealing in our discussions. The term includes the whole self as it consists of the self that you know, probable selves, reincarnated selves, and selves more highly developed than the self that you know.", "source": "早期课第7册 · 第309节", "year": "1968", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第7册", "session": "309", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0025", "zh": "你曾斟酌过而没用到的概念，也可以被其他的可能自己以同样的方式拣到。自然，每一个可能的自己都认为自己才是真正的“你”，而对他们任一个而言，你也就是那一个可能的自己；但透过“内在感官”你们全都知觉到你们是这整个完形的一部分。", "en": "Ideas that you have entertained and not used may be picked up in this same manner by other probable you’s. Each of these probable selves consider themselves the real you, of course, and to any one of them you would be the probable self; but through the inner senses all of you are aware of your part in this gestalt.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第565节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "565", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0049", "zh": "基本上，自己没有局限，而自己的所有部分全是相连的——因此，可能的自己们是无意识地觉察到他们的关系的。", "en": "Now: There are basically no limitations to the self, and all portions of the self are connected — so the probable selves are aware, unconsciously, of their relationships.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第680节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "680", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0074", "zh": "我试着很强烈的声明个人的原始独特性，我也说过你自己是没有限制的，这两个声明可以显得是彼此矛盾的。当你是一个小孩时，在你一般的经验里你的身分感并不包括老年的你。当你是一个老人时，你不认同自己为一个小孩。那么，你的身分感随着年岁具体的改变。以某种方式而言，好像你透过经验而丰富了你自己，变得“比以前的你更多了”。你进出于可能的自己，而却在同时——常常毫不费力的——维持住一个你自己的身分。意识的马赛克拼图真是绚丽耀目。", "en": "I try to strongly state the pristine uniqueness of the individual. I also say that there are no limitations to the self. The two statements can appear to be contradictory. When you are a child, your sense of identity does not include old age in usual experience. When you are an old person, you do not identify yourself as a child. Your sense of identity, then, changes physically through the years. In a way it seems that you add on to yourself through experience, becoming “more than you were before.” You move in and out of probable selfhoods, while at the same time — usually with the greatest of ease — you maintain an identity of yourself. The mosaics of consciousness are brilliant to behold.", "source": "个人与群体事件的本质 · 第834节", "year": "1977–79", "tag": null, "short": "个人与群体事件的本质", "session": "834", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B04", "title": "你现在这个“官方的自己”，是挑出来的", "note": "这一块的枢纽是赛斯那句：你出生时的私人蓝图，比它能在你的空间与时间里发生的任何一种物质具体化都大得多，它给你选择的范围、给你可操纵性；当你的概念改变、当你移向一个可能的自己并决定把它当作你官方的自己时，你永远有一个丰富的可能行为库藏可选——如果只给你一个，你就根本没有选择了。他把同一条逻辑放大到看起来最结实的地方：你有你认可的官方个人过去，你们也多少接受了一个官方的集体历史，可在检视之下，那族类的历史露出许多空隙与不一致，留下许多待答的问题；私人有可能的自己，族类一样有。他也说其他的可能本体就与你现称为自己的这个本体并存——是并存，不是排队的先后。对普通人最要紧的是另一句：在你目前的存在里，你只使用那些你相信自己拥有的心理特性，所以人格是不能被定义成“就这样”的。", "src": "第 565、653、695、697 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 565 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 653 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 695 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 697 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-104-e0019", "zh": "就如鲁曼尼亚的一些特质仍然存在于你们的心灵大气里，就如他们的城市仍然与你们现称为自己的土地的地区同时并存，因而其他的可能本体也与你现称为自己的这个本体并存。在下一章我们将讨论你和你可能的自己。", "en": "As the various qualities of the Lumanians are still present in your psychic atmosphere, as their cities still coexist in land areas now called your own, so other probable identities coexist with the identities you now call your own. In the following chapter we will discuss you and your probable selves.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第565节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "565", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0041", "zh": "因为你心理与心灵的结构，所以在你存在的丰富构造之内，真的是有无穷尽的变化，你可称之为“可能的自己”。在一个或另一个实相里，这些全部都会被经历到。然而，在你目前的存在里，你只会用那些你相信自己拥有的心理特性，因此，人格是不能下定义的。", "en": "Because of your psychological and psychic structure, there is within the rich makeup of your being a literally endless variety of what you may call probable selves. In one reality or another these will all be experienced. In your present existence however you will utilize only those psychological characteristics that you believe you possess. So, you see, the personality cannot be defined as being thus-and-so.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第653节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "653", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0060", "zh": "当你在看着你个人历史里的一张照片时，那代表了你在这个特定实相——或在它被拍下来时，被接受为官方的那个实相——里的浮出，所以，你是在看着一张代表你们族类，在一个可能性的特定刹那被攫住的照片，那个族类有如你私底下所有的许多分支与发展。就私人而言，有可能的自己们，就族类而言，也有可能的自己们，正如你有你认可的官方个人过去，所以在你们的实际系统里，你们也多少接受了一个官方的集体历史。然而，在检视之下，那族类的历史显出了许多的空隙及不一致之处，而留下许多有待回答的问题。", "en": "As you are looking at one photograph in your personal history, that represents your emergence in this particular reality — or the reality that was accepted as official at the time it was taken — so you are looking at a picture of a representative of your species, caught in a particular moment of probability. That species has as many offshoots and developments as you have privately. As there are probable selves in private terms, there are probable selves in terms of the species. As you have your recognized, official personal past, so in your system of actuality you have more or less accepted an official mass history (see Note 2). Under examination, however, that history of the species shows many gaps and discrepancies, and it leaves many questions to be answered.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第695节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "695", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0061", "zh": "你在出生时的私人蓝图，在某种说法上是比能发生在你们的空间与时间里它的任何一个实质具体化远为大得多，这提供了你选择的范围，给了你可操纵性，并且让种种的可能活动“可能”。就那方面而言，你是那裁判及决定者，所以，当你的概念改变时，当你移向一个可能的自己，而决定将它当作你官方的自己时，你会永远有一个可供选择的可能行为之丰富库藏。如果只提供了一个，你就没有选择了，这也同样适用于这族类。 现在，给我们一会儿……", "en": "The private blueprint, yours at birth, is in certain terms far greater than any one physical materialization of it that could occur in your space and time. This provides you with areas of choice, gives you manipulability, and allows for the myriad of probable activities “possible.” You are the judge and the final word in that regard, so that as your ideas change, as you move toward one probable self and decide upon that as your official3 self, you will always have a rich bank of probable actions to choose from. If only one were provided you would have no choice. The same applies to the species. Now give us a moment …", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第697节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "697", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B05", "title": "梦、灵感、突然冒上来的念头", "note": "赛斯给灵感一个非常具体的讲法：在你看来像是灵感的这个东西，常常是另一个自己想过却没有实现的思想，你调准频率收到它、替它把它实现。这不是玄谈，他指出了通道：你与其他可能的自己之间有自然的吸引力，是与能量同时推进有关的电磁性关联——同一份能量同时出现在你和别的实相里的你身上，所有的你之间有一种统一的、交感的、情绪上的反应，而这种联系在梦境里特别明显。于是他敢说：一个关于不幸事件的梦把你吓得半死，可能是一个真的经历了那件事的可能的自己捎来的讯息，你因此避开了它；一个孩子也可能在梦里收到来自可能的未来自己的讯息，性质强烈到一生被改变，因为整个本体当下就存在着，所有的划分只是幻觉，一个可能的自己就能朝另一个伸出援手。他也说你能按自己的性向、心灵的柔软度与好奇心，接收到一个可能的自己正在经历的事件，从而确知你的意识与身份独立于你此刻聚焦的这个世界。你们那些在深层感知到彼此临在的时刻，思绪会离开惯常的轨道沿切线滑出去——他就举过一例：闪过一个人体内部的形象，那是接到自己另一个版本的在场。同一时间你还能做不止一个梦，也能体验可能的自己的梦的版本，但总有接触点：你收到那样的梦，总有一个理由。", "src": "第 487、531、565、566、903 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 487 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 531 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 565 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 566 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 903 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-104-e0011", "zh": "那时你们的内在思绪离开惯常的轨道，沿着切线滑了出去。我想，你们见过一个人体内部部分的形象，或者闪过一个与内脏有关的念头。这是因为你们在一个深层上感知到了你们可能的自己的临在。", "en": "It was a time when your inner thoughts veered off at a tangent from their familiar ways. I believe you had an image of the inside portion of a human body, or a thought having to do with inner organs. This happened as you picked up on a deep level the presence of your probable self.", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第487节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "487", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0018", "zh": "这些意识流中可能有一些会涉及了你所谓的一个“转世的自己”的思想，贯注于你所谓的另一个历史时期中。相反的，有时候，按照你的性向、你心灵的柔软度、你的好奇心、你的求知欲，你可以“接收到”一个“可能的自己”所涉及的事件。换言之，你可能开始知觉到一个比你现在所知的远为伟大的实相，开始利用那你还不知道你所拥有的能力，并且确知无疑：你自己的意识与本体是独立于你主要的注意力贯注在其中的这个世界。如果以上所有我说的不是真的，那么我不会在写这本书，你也不会在读它。", "en": "Some of these may involve the thoughts of what you would call a reincarnational self, focused in another period of history as you know it. You may instead, “pick up” an event in which a probable self is involved, according to your inclination, your psychic suppleness, your curiosity, your desire for knowledge. In other words, you may become aware of a far greater reality than you now know, use abilities that you do not realize you possess, know beyond all doubt that your own consciousness and identity is independent of the world in which you now focus your primary attention. If all of that were not true, I would not be writing this book and you would not be reading it.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第531节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "531", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0024", "zh": "按照你开放与接受的程度，你可由“可能的自己”的各种经验获得极大的好处，也可以从可能的自己所拥有的知识与才能而获益。你也常常十分自发地在梦境中这样做，而在你看来像是一个灵感的东西，常常是另一个自己想过却没有实现的思想。你调准频率后收到了它而把它给实现了，你明白吗？", "en": "To the extent that you are open and receptive, you can benefit greatly by the various experiences of your probable selves, and can gain from their knowledge and abilities. Quite spontaneously, again, you often do this in the dream state, and often what seems to you to be an inspiration is a thought experienced but not actualized on the part of another self. You tune in and actualize it instead, you see.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第565节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "565", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0030", "zh": "那么，你发出概念，也收到概念，但你并没有被迫地去实现那些由其他可能的自己而来的未实现的可能的行为。且说，在你自己与其他可能的自己之间有种自然的吸引力，与能量的同时推进有关的电磁性关联。我这是指对你和在其他实相中的可能的你同时出现的能量；在所有的“你”之间有一种心灵上的关系，彼此有一种统一的、交感的、情感上的反应，这种联系在梦境中非常明显。", "en": "You originate ideas then and receive them, but you are not forced to actualize unrealized probable acts that come to you from other probable selves. Now there is a natural attraction between yourself and other probable selves, electromagnetic connections having to do with simultaneous propulsions of energy. By this I mean energy that appears simultaneously both to you and probable selves in other realities; psychic connections having to do with a uniting, sympathetic, emotional reaction and a connection that shows up very strongly in the dream state.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第566节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "566", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0033", "zh": "因为在各个“可能的自己”之间彼此有“渗漏”与交互关联，调准到“未来的事件”对你而言是可能的，那么假设你因循你现在的生活方向，你可能会碰上一件不幸的事情。但是你可以借由调准到这未来事件中，而避开了那件事情，没有去实际地经历它。比如，你可能作了一个关于那事情的梦而吓得半死，但这样的一个梦乃是由确实已经去经历这个事件的一个可能的自己所捎来的讯息。", "en": "Because there are bleed-throughs and interconnections, it is possible for you to tune into a “future event,” say of an unfortunate nature, an event for which you are headed if you continue on your present course. A dream about it, for instance, may so frighten you that you avoid the event and do not experience it. If so, such a dream is a message from a probable self who did experience the event.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第566节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "566", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0034", "zh": "那么一个孩子因此可能在一个梦里收到由可能的未来的自己所送来的讯息，这讯息具有这样一种性质，以致完全改变了他的一生。整个的本体“当下”就存在，所有的划分只是幻觉，因此一个可能性的自己能对另一个伸出援手，而经由这些内在沟通，你们所谓的各个可能的自己乃开始了解他们的本体本质为何。", "en": "So can a child then in a dream receive such communications from a probable future self, of such a nature that its life is completely changed. The entire identity is being now. All divisions are merely illusions, so one probable self can hold out a helping hand to another, and through these inner communications the various probable selves in your terms begin to understand the nature of their identity.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第566节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "566", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0075", "zh": "你知道，在一个时候你可以有不只一个梦。你也可以体验可能自己的梦的版本，但总会有一些接触点——那是说，你为什么会收到这样的一个梦总有一个理由。人们所有的梦形成了一个集体的梦架构。梦存在于其他的层面，而当然，在物质层面它们影响身体的状态。以这种方式，世界的行动是在集体的梦沟通里解决的，那在同时既是公共的又是私人的。", "en": "You know you can have more than one dream at a time. You can also experience versions of dreams of probable selves, but there will always be some point of contact—that is, there will always be a reason why you pick up such a dream. All of the dreams people have form a mass dream framework. Dreams exist at other levels, and physically of course they affect the body state. In such ways, the world’s actions are worked out in mass dream communications that are at the same time public and private.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第903节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "903", "only_en": true}]}]}, {"id": "B06", "title": "你能借他们的才能，他们也能接你的念头", "note": "赛斯说一件事可以被不止一个可能的自己实现，而你与某些可能的自己比与另一些更相像；正因为你涉身于这样一个复杂的心理完形，又确实存在前面说的心灵联系，你多少能借用你人格这些其他部分所有的才能与知识。他说痕迹本来就在：你目前的经验里有你的可能的自己的痕迹，就像每个人身上都有那几个把伟大才能灿烂发展的人所显示的记号——这些痕迹可以被带进你的经验来丰富它，而无论如何，在无意识层面他们已经是这么在做了，在那儿他们形成了你选择目前经验的基础。知识也不作废：你获得的不只被你想的那些个别自己保留着，也变成其他实相里所有可能的自己的知识的一部分。同一个思想会被你各个可能的自己以各自的方式体验，可能被拒绝、可能转瞬即逝，然后形成其他行动事件——你在这个系统里创造的，在别的系统里也有作用。他还说在出体状态里许多人接触过可能的自己与可能的实相，而私人的心灵本身就在其内包含着对自己可能性的知识。", "src": "第 342、489、499、566、655、709 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 342 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 489 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 499 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 566 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 655 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 709 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-104-e0005", "zh": "要知道，你所经历的思想，也可以被你各个可能的自己透过不同的方式体验。他们会以特有的方式做出反应，以自己的方式体验思想，这些方式可能都与你自己的截然不同。", "en": "A thought experienced by you is also experienced by your probable selves, you see, in one way or another. They will react in their characteristic way, and experience the thought in their own manner, which may be quite different than your own.", "source": "早期课第8册 · 第342节", "year": "1969", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第8册", "session": "342", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0006", "zh": "思想可能会被拒绝，而总是会有某个可以拒绝的理由。对于任何可能的自己而言，这个思想可能相对无意义，于是转瞬即逝，可能的自己会以某种方式做出反应，而形成其他行动事件。持续的创造力适用于每个系统，你们在一个系统中所创造的东西，也会影响到其他系统。", "en": "The thought may be rejected, but there will be something to reject. The thought may be relatively meaningless to any given probable self and it may be very fleeting. The probable selves in one way or another will react, forming other action events. Constant creativity applies to every system, then, and what you create in one system has its effects in other systems also.", "source": "早期课第8册 · 第342节", "year": "1969", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第8册", "session": "342", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0012", "zh": "你们同时也在从其他可能的自己那里接收讯息。你们在筹划尘世的转世。你们在建立那些将于其他领域派上用场的联络。所以，你们闭上眼睛时，便把实相带到地上来；睁开眼睛时，又把它们推进实相之中。", "en": "You are also receiving information from other probable selves. You are planning earthly reincarnations. You are making contacts that will be used in other realms. When your eyes close therefore, you bring realities to earth, and when your eyes open you also thrust them out into actuality.", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第489节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "489", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0015", "zh": "现在。我告诉过你们，所获得的知识会被保留。不止如此，它不仅被你们所想的那些个别的自己保留着。它也变成其他实相里所有可能的自己的知识的一部分。", "en": "Now. I told you that the knowledge gained would be retained. Not only this, but it is retained not only by the individual selves as you think of them. It becomes part of the knowledge of all the probable selves in other realities also.", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第499节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "499", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0031", "zh": "然而，一件事可以被不止一个可能的自己所实现，而你与某些可能的自己比另一些可能的自己更相像。因为你涉身于这样复杂的一个“心理的完形”里，又因先前我所说过的确实存在的心灵联系，你能借用一些你人格的这些其他可能部分的才能与知识。", "en": "Now: One event can be actualized by more than one probable self, however, and you will resemble some probable selves more than others. Because you are involved in an intricate psychological gestalt such as this, and because the connections mentioned earlier do exist, you can avail yourself to some extent of abilities and knowledge possessed by these other probable portions of your personality.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第566节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "566", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0042", "zh": "在你目前的经验里，有你“可能自己”的痕迹，就如即使在每一个个人里，都有那些少数几个人所显示的且如此灿烂发展的伟大才能的记号。这些痕迹可以被带到你的经验里，来丰富你的人生，但无论如何，在无意识层面里，可能的自己已经是这样在做了，在那儿，他们形成了你选择目前经验的基础。", "en": "There are traces in your present experience of your probable selves, even as there are signs in each individual of all the great talents shown and developed so flamboyantly by a few. These traces can be brought into your experience to enrich it. They do so in any case on unconscious levels, where they form the basis from which you choose your current experience.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第655节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "655", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0063", "zh": "人类身体本身具有无限的潜力，以及了不起的变量，那容许许多不同种类的取向。从你们的观点，可能的人代表了替代的人，即这族类的替代版本。这同样也适用于个人。在出体状态许多人曾接触到可能的自己及可能的实相，他们也曾旅游到你们所认为的过去与未来。私人的心灵在其本身内包含着对其自己的可能性之知识，而它包含了一个镜子，在其中，至少可以对人类的经验略见一瞥。", "en": "The human body itself has limitless potentials, and great variations that allow for different kinds of orientation. Probable man represents alternate man from your viewpoint, alternate versions of the species. The same applies individually. In out-of-body states many people have encountered probable selves and probable realities. They have also journeyed into the past and the future as you think of them. The private psyche contains within itself the knowledge of its own probabilities, and it contains a mirror in which the experience of the species can at least be glimpsed.", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第709节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "709", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B07", "title": "遗憾和罪恶感为什么站不住", "note": "这一页最解放人的一点在这里：如果没被采用的那条路其实有另一个你真的在走，遗憾和罪恶感就都没了立足处——事情不是消失了，是被你的另一个部分接过去活完了。赛斯同时也把往回看做成一件有后果的操作：他说沉湎于过去的不愉快是非常不智的，因为可能的自己的某个部分或许仍然卷入在那个过去里；对过去集中注意可能带来更多的渗漏与不利的认同，因为那个过去是你和从那个特定源头跃出的任何可能的自己的一个共同背景。他给的动作又朴素得可以：拿两张不同年纪的照片，问自己是在看同一个人吗，想想在一张里想到、在另一张里没被跟随的那些路子——那些方向的确被追随了，如果不是被你认知的自己，那就是被一个可能的自己；这种带着欲望的沉思、由常识支撑着，能带来在可能性里的交会点，引起心灵深层成分一次新鲜的重新排列。他也点出人为什么不敢想这件事：好像手上有一个无限的才能库藏却害怕去取，怕任何增加会让你变少而不是变多。他举的例子特别世俗：一个搞文学的人四十岁忽然记起旧日的木工爱好，自己动手修家具，多年蔑视这类活动之后，忽然发现与土地及其物产的亲密关系，而这份欣赏增益了原本可能味同嚼蜡的字句。", "src": "第 566、695、735 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 566 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 695 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 735 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-104-e0032", "zh": "要知道，如果这冲动是来自一个可能的自己，你会更快地学会这乐器。那么不用说，可能的自己当然也存在于你的“将来”如同存在于你的过去一样。沉缅于你过去的不愉快里是非常不智的，因为可能的自己的某个部分或许仍然卷入在那个过去里。对过去集中注意可能会有更多的“渗漏”与不利的认同，因为那个“过去”是你和由那特定源头所跃出的任何可能自己的一个“共同背景”。", "en": "You would learn the instrument far quicker, you see, if the impulse was originating with a probable self. It goes without saying then that probable selves exist in your “future” as well as your past. It is very poor policy to dwell negatively on unpleasant aspects of the past that you know, because some portions of the probable self may still be involved in that past. The concentration can allow greater bleed-through and adverse identification, because that part will be one background that you have in common with any probable selves who sprang from that particular source.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第566节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "566", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0058", "zh": "练习单元4 现在：拿另一张与刚才那张不同年龄的照片，只简单的问你自己：“我是在看同样一个人吗？”这第二张照片有多熟悉或多陌生？它与你今晚选的第一张有何不同？有什么相似性？把这两张照片在你脑海里联合起来，在每张照片被拍下时你有什么样的经验？在一张照片里你想到要跟随，而在那另一张里没被跟随的有那些路子？那些路子的确是被追随的，如果它们没被你所认知的自己追随的话，那么它们也的确被一个以你们的话来说可能的自己追随。当你想到这种事件时，在你的脑子里跟随着那个自己可能会采取的方向，如果你找到一条你现在希望你曾追随但却未追随的发展路线的话，那么深深的去想，那些活动现在可能锲入你官方地被接受的生活之架构的方式。这种带着欲望的沉思——由常识支撑着，能带来在可能性里的交会点，那可引起心灵深层成分的一个新鲜的重新排列。以这种方式，可能事件可以被吸引到你目前的生活结构里。", "en": "Now: Take another photograph of yourself at a different age than the first one you chose. Ask yourself simply: “Am I looking at the same person?” How familiar or how strange is this second photograph? How does it differ from the first one you picked this evening? What similarities are there that unite both photographs in your mind? What experiences did you have when each photograph was taken? What ways did you think of following in one picture that were not followed in the other one? Those directions were pursued. If they were not pursued by the self you recognize, then they were by a self that is probable in your terms. In your mind follow what directions that self would have taken, as you think of such events. If you find a line of development that you now wish you had pursued, but had not, then think deeply about the ways in which those activities could now fit into the framework of your officially accepted life.2 Such musings, with desire — backed up by common sense — can bring about intersection points in probabilities that cause a fresh realignment of the deep elements of the psyche. In such ways probable events can be attracted to your current living structure.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第695节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "695", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0072", "zh": "当你接触到可能的自己或对等人物的观念时，到某个程度，你也有同样的感受。就像是你有一个可以由之汲取能力与特性之一个无限的库藏，但却害怕去这样做——害怕任何的增加会令你变少而非变多。如果所有这些在个人层面上进行的话——当你从那库藏里选择一个旋律而称它为你自己时——那么，也许你可以开始看到仿佛有起有落的文明之群体的创造面。", "en": "To some degree you feel the same way when you encounter the concept of probable selves, or of counterparts. It is as though you had an unlimited bank of abilities and characteristics from which to draw, and yet were afraid of doing so — fearing that any addition could make you less instead of more. If all of this goes on personally, as you choose one melody and call it yourself, then perhaps you can begin to see the mass creative aspects in terms of civilizations that seem to rise and fall.", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第735节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "735", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0073", "zh": "在最后的几句里，有与你所知的自己相关的可能的自己、转世的自己以及对等人物之一个直觉的“定义”。可是，在你的例子里，如果你选择如此做的话，你可以改变你自己的速度，加上变奏，甚或开始一个全新的曲子。现在，许多人借着突然的决定利用那些他们先前忽略的能力，而以非常简单且世俗的方式做到了此点。比如说，一个搞文学的人在四十岁时突然记起他对木工的旧爱，阅读“自己动手做”手册，而开始他自己的家具修理。在多年来蔑视这种活动为可鄙之后，他忽然发现了与土地及其物产的一个亲密关系，而这种欣赏增益了原本可能味同嚼蜡的字句。", "en": "In the last few sentences there is an intuitive “definition” of probable and reincarnational selves, and counterparts, in relationship to the self that you know. In your case, however, you can change your own pacing, add variations, or even begin an entirely new composition if you choose to. Now many people have done this in very simple, mundane ways by suddenly deciding to use abilities they had earlier ignored. A man of letters, for instance, at the age of 40 suddenly remembers his old love of carpentry, reads do-it-yourself manuals, and begins his own home repairs. After disdaining such activities as beneath him for years, he suddenly discovers an intimate relationship with earth and its goods, and this appreciation adds to words that before may have been as dry as ash.", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第735节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "735", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B08", "title": "你的灵魂本来就包括他们", "note": "赛斯把可能的自己算进灵魂的定义里，不是当外挂：就像有可能的人，也有可能的神，而这些可能的神全是你称为一切万有的那个灵魂或本体的一部分，正如你的可能的自己全是你灵魂或本体的一部分。他说以某种方式你曾透过自己的思想与欲望创造了许多可能的神，他们成为相当独立的心灵本体，在其他的存在次元里实现；那唯一的一切万有知觉的不只是它自己的本质与所有意识的本质，也知觉它那无限量的可能的自己——再往下讲，他自己就说文字开始变得无意义。他顺手把“进步”也改掉：他不喜欢求进步这个说法，但作为一个意识，进步就是越来越知觉到你自己本体的那些其他具体化，让可能的自己获得对其他可能的自己的觉知，而了悟到所有这些都是真正本体的种种不同显现。讲他自己也一样：他的转世人格、可能的自己甚至赛斯第二现在都存在于他内、他存在于他们内，他对可能的自己跟对转世的人生一样有知觉，然后补一句——你们对灵魂的观念根本就太过狭隘。给罗和珍画像那节课他也说，那两张不是过去人格的像，而是整个自己的图像表征，这些整个人格知晓你们当下的存在、在你们睡眠时给你们忠告，而你们可能的自己同样是你存有一的一部分。整体人格由你知道的自己、可能的自己、转世的自己组成，对它而言死亡根本不存在，只有你当中那些物质导向的部分接受那是终结；当你用到对你来说超乎正常的能力，你就是在从最高的自己汲取。最后他把这一整串拉回家常：死后仍活着是自然的，把身体还诸大地再形成一个是自然的，你拥有可能的自己与转生的存在，同样是自然的。", "src": "第 301、507、565、566、589、631 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 301 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 507 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 565 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 566 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 589 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 631 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-104-e0001", "zh": "对整体人格而言，死亡根本不存在。只有自己当中那些物质导向的部分接受这是终结。无论何时，只要你使用对你来说似乎超乎正常的能力，你就是从最高的自己汲取能力。它是整体的我（whole I），但又不只是它所有部分的总和。它是高度觉知的行动，而且颇有能力改变它的成分。当然它也是由可能的自己组成的，这我们谈过了，而且它统一并指挥行动的更大部分，无限大到肉身人格（physical personality）不可能应付的部分。", "en": "Death simply does not exist to the whole personality. Only the physically oriented portions of the self accept this as a finality. (Long pause.) Whenever you use abilities that to you seem supernormal, you are drawing upon the ability of the supraself. It is the whole I and yet more than the sum of its parts. It is action highly aware, and quite able to change its components. It consists also of course of the probable selves of which we have spoken, and it unites and directs infinitely larger portions of action than possible for the physical personality.", "source": "早期课第7册 · 第301节", "year": "1968", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第7册", "session": "301", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0017", "zh": "这幅肖像是约瑟[Joseph]与鲁柏。用你们的话说，这并非你们作为过去人格的画像，也不是某几个特定转世自己的画像。它们是你们所是的那整个自己的图像表征——用你们的话说，便是那整个自己：你们全部转世人格的总和。这些整个自己，接着是你们存有一的一部分；然而，你们可能的自己同样也是你们存有一的一部分。倘若你们愿意按“向导”来设想——加引号的“天使”——那么你们便触及了这些名相背后的实相。鲁柏和约瑟，是我用来称呼你们的名字，因为这些名字本身就意味着你们更宏大的目标。这些整个人格知晓你们当下的存在，并在你们处于睡眠状态时给你们忠告。它们是力量的源泉。你们可以汲取他们的知识，也可以汲取你们自家转世自己的知识——那加引号的“过去”与“未来”。", "en": "The portrait is of Joseph and Ruburt. These are not portraits of yourselves as past personalities in your terms, or of particular re-incarnational selves. They are pictorial representatives of the whole selves that you are. In your terms the selves that are the sum of your re-incarnational personalities. These whole selves then are a part of your entity. Your probable selves are also a part of your entity, however. If you want to think in terms of guides, in quotes “angels,” then you have the reality behind those terms. Ruburt and Joseph are the names I use for you, for these names themselves imply your greater goals. These personalities are aware of your present existence, and give you advice when you are in the sleep state. They are a source of strength. You can draw upon their knowledge, and you can also draw upon the knowledge of your own re-incarnational selves, past, in quotes, and future.", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第507节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "507", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0026", "zh": "事实上它根本不能说是个作品，而是“变为”的一个过程。“一切万有”也不是个已完成或未完成的产品。同样的就如同有可能的人，也有可能的神；但这些可能的神全部都是你称之为“一切万有”的灵魂或本体的一部分，正如你的可能的自己全都是你的灵魂或本体的一部分。", "en": "In fact it is not a product in those terms at all, but a process of becoming. All That Is is not a product, finished or otherwise, either. There are probable gods as there are probable men; but these probable gods are all a part of what you may call the soul of, or the identity of, All That Is; even as your probable selves are all a portion of your soul or entity.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第565节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "565", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0027", "zh": "当然，“一切万有”可能在其中获致实现的次元远远超过你现在所能得到的。以一种方式来说，你曾透过你的思想与欲望创造了许多可能的神。他们变成了相当独立的心灵本体，在其他的存在次元里实现。这个唯一的“一切万有”所知觉的，不止是它自己的本质与所有意识的本质，它也知觉它那无限量的可能的自己。由这里我们再谈下去的话，所谈的主题已经无法以文字来描述了，所有的话都会变得无意义了。", "en": "The dimensions of actuality possible to All That Is of course far exceed those presently available to you. In a manner of speaking, you have created many probable gods through your own thoughts and desires. They become quite independent psychic entities, validities in other levels of existence. The one All That Is is aware not only of its own nature and of the nature of all consciousness, but is also aware of its infinite probable selves. We go here toward subjects in which words become meaningless.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第565节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "565", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0028", "zh": "我不喜欢“求进步”这句话，然而以你们来说，作为一个意识，“进步”就是变得越来越知觉到你自己本体的那些其他的具体化。可能的自己就是要获知其他的可能的自已，而了悟到所有的都是“真正本体”的种种不同的显现。", "en": "I do not like the phrase, “to advance”, yet in your terms “to advance” as a consciousness is to become more and more aware of these other materializations of your own identity. The probable selves are to gain awareness of the other probable selves, and realize that all are various manifestations of the true identity.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第566节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "566", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0037", "zh": "我自己的转世的人格、可能的自己、甚至“赛斯第二”现在都存在于我内，而我存在于他们内。以你们的话来说，赛斯第二是更高深的。以你们的话来说，他也是更陌生的，既然他无法像我这样与你们人间生活扯上关系，因为我在人世有深厚背景。", "en": "My own reincarnational personalities, probable selves, and even Seth Two exist within me now, as I exist within them. In your terms, Seth Two is more advanced. In your terms, he is more alien, since he cannot relate to your physical existence as well as I do because of my background in it.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第589节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "589", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0038", "zh": "广义地说，我的灵魂包括我的转世人格、赛斯第二及可能的自己。附带地说，我对我可能的自己与对我转世的人生都一样有知觉。你们对灵魂的观念根本就太过狭隘。我并不是真的在谈集体灵魂的观点，虽然也可以这样诠释。", "en": "In larger terms, my soul includes my reincarnational personalities, Seth Two, and probable selves. I am as aware of my probable selves, incidentally, as I am of my reincarnational existences. Your concept of the soul is simply so limited. I am not really speaking in terms of group souls, though this interpretation can also be made.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第589节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "589", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0040", "zh": "死后仍活着是自然的，把身体还诸大地而后再形成另一个是自然的。你的思想和病毒一样地迅速、有反应而活生生也是自然的。你拥有“可能的自己”和“转生的存在”②也是自然的。", "en": "It is natural to live after death, and natural to return the body to earth and [then to] form another. It is natural for your thoughts to be as quick, responsive, and alive as viruses. It is natural for you to have probable selves as well as reincarnational existences.*", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第631节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "631", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B09", "title": "跟转世的自己、对等人物怎么分得开", "note": "名词一多就会打结，赛斯自己承认这一点：他用了很多名词，可能看起来很难了解可能的自己与转世的自己、对等人物、意识的家族之间有什么不同，你也许觉得奇怪在这么多种心灵变奏之间你如何做你自己。他的答复分两层。一层是它们同时成立：你问如何能同时有转世的自己与可能的自己，就像在问一个苹果怎么能同时有颜色又是圆的。另一层是他把层级摆正：转世的自己只不过是选择去体验各种不同形式的可能自己；你的转世自己和你一样多有可能的生命，你在当下的信念与行动改变了它们的经验，正如它们每一个在它们的当下也改变你的——若把这些转世的自己看做一个存在，这种彼此影响就变得十分自然，整个自己被它所有的理解所改变。他还说就你所知的心灵就是由这些意识家族的一种混合组成，其一并不比另一个更高超，它们只是不同，代表形形色色看待物质生命的方式；也因为基本单位那种了不起的组织天性，存在一些颇能维持住自己身份、同时觉察任何数目的可能自己的心理结构。最后是他给这套名词的定调：在你存在的真正交响乐里，你是小提琴、双簧管、铙钹及竖琴，你是一个活生生的乐器，透过它你演奏你自己；你不是一个被演奏的乐器，你是作曲家及交响乐团。", "src": "第 343、668、682、735 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 343 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 668 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 682 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 735 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-104-e0007", "zh": "比如说，他可以在里面继续几次的转世，转世的自己只不过是可能的自己，选择去体验各种不同的形式。在这种情况下，就你们来说，人格不会离开你们系统一段时间，尽管这都是主观的。要知道，体验是唯一的现实。", "en": "He may, for example, continue within it for several reincarnations. Reincarnated selves are no more than probable selves, choosing to experience various forms. In such circumstances, the personality does not leave your system, in your terms, for some time, though this is all subjective. Experience is the only reality, you see.", "source": "早期课第8册 · 第343节", "year": "1969", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第8册", "session": "343", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0043", "zh": "你“转世的自己”就与你有一样多的“可能的自己”。你在当下的信念与行动改变了“它们的”经验，就如它们每一个在当下里也改变了你的经验一样，如果你把这些“转世的自己”看做一个“存在”，那么，这种彼此的影响就变得十分自然，全部被所有它自己的“理解”改变了。", "en": "Your reincarnational selves have as many probable lives as you do. Your beliefs and actions in your present alter “their” experiences, as each of them, in their presents, change yours. If you see these reincarnational selves as one entity, then this becomes quite natural. The whole self is changed by all of its comprehensions.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第668节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "668", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0053", "zh": "因为这些基本单位了不起的组织天性，所以也有一些“心理结构”，它们颇能维持住它们自己的身份，同时却也能觉察任何数目的可能自己。在你们的实相里，死后的生命大有意义，因为死亡是你们实相的一部分。你更大的实相显然是超越了你的生及你的死二者。单独一个宇宙的概念基本上是荒诞无稽的。你们的实相必须在它与其他实相的关系里被看待，不然的话，你就永远会被像“宇宙是怎么开始的？”或“它什么时候会结束？”这种问题所攫获。所有的系统经常不断的在被创造。", "en": "Because of the great organizing nature of these basic units, there are also psychological structures that are quite capable of holding their own identities while being aware of any given number of probable selves. Life after death has great meaning in your reality, because death is a part of it. Your greater reality obviously transcends both your births and your deaths. The idea of one universe alone is basically nonsensical. Your reality must be seen in its relationship to others.8 Otherwise you are always caught in questions like “How did the universe begin?” or “When will it end?” All systems are constantly being created.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第682节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "682", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0069", "zh": "那么，如你所知的心灵是由这种意识家族的一个混合所组成的。其一并不比另一个更高超。它们只不过是不同而已，而它们代表了形形色色看待物质生命的方式。（停顿。）要解释关于不同意识家族的心灵次元需要一整本书，此地在这本书里我仅仅想要使读者知道有这些心灵集团的存在。我非常明白我用了很多的名词，而可能看起来很难了解在可能的自己与转世的自己、对等人物与意识的家族之间的不同。有时候矛盾可能仿佛存在。你也许觉得奇怪，在如此多种类的心灵“变奏”之间你又如何做你自己。", "en": "The psyche as you know it, then, is composed of a mixture of these families of consciousness. One is not superior to the others. They are just different, and they represent various ways of looking at physical life. (Pause.) A book would be needed to explain the dimensions of the psyche in relation to the different families of consciousness. Here, in this manuscript, I merely want to make the reader aware of the existence of these psychic groupings. I am alert to the fact that I am using many terms, and that it may seem difficult to understand the differences between probable and reincarnational selves, counterparts and families of consciousness. At times contradictions may seem to exist. You may wonder how you are you in the midst of such multitudinous psychic “variations.”", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第735节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "735", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0070", "zh": "可是，意识的内在次元没有办法如此容易的描写。如果你问：“我如何能同时有转世的自己与可能的自己？”你是在问类似先前提及的一个问题：“一个苹果怎么能同时有颜色又是圆的呢？”", "en": "(Occasionally Seth will make this kind of correction in his material.) The inside dimensions of consciousness cannot be so easily described, however. If you ask: “How can I have reincarnational and probable selves at once?”, you are asking a question comparable to the one mentioned earlier, colon: “How can an apple have color and be round at the same time?”", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第735节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "735", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0071", "zh": "那么，当我说到对等人物或转世的自己及可能的自己时，我说的是在你存在的真正交响乐里，你是小提琴、双簧管、铙钹及竖琴——换言之，你是一个活生生的乐器，透过它你演奏你自己。你并不是一个被演奏的乐器，反之，你是作曲家及交响乐团。你奏民歌、古典曲目、抒情曲及歌剧，然而，一个创造性的表演并不与其它的抵触。", "en": "When I speak in terms of counterparts, then, or of reincarnational selves and probable selves, I am saying that in the true symphony of your being you are violins, oboes, cymbals, harps — in other words, you are a living instrument through which you play yourself. You are not an instrument upon which you are played. You are the composer and the symphony. You play ballads, classical pieces, lyrics, operas. One creative performance does not contradict the others.", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第735节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "735", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B10", "title": "赛斯那一侧：他看得见什么，他自己也有", "note": "这一页有几段是赛斯从他那一侧说的，值得一读，因为它们划出这套说法到底能到哪里。他说珍透过他的记忆成长，就像一棵树穿过空间生长，她一变他的记忆也跟着变，而他对她的记忆包括她的可能的自己——所有这些坐标同时存在于一个不占用空间的点上。他也给过一个坦率的能力边界：他知觉一个房间里的人们，与他们知觉自己非常不同，他们的各种前生与来世的人格对他可知觉，可能的自己却不可——所以这一页不承诺任何人能随时清点你有多少个自己。同一批课里他又说，你们那些可能的自己，他和像他这样的存在都看得见，因为他们看见你们实相的一切维度，而你们只从你们所认识的那些小小的当下形态去看它；还有一节课他记下两度几乎达成的接触，对方是他在另一个更为先进的实相中的可能自己，所发生的是一次在深于意识的层面上的极短暂融合。最贴身的例子是他讲罗的父亲：你们所认识的那位父亲是那可能的自己——那创造性的、有机械发明才能的人格开始超过了另一个，而那可能的自己处理的正是另一个所避开的情感实相，这的确是他唯一的意图。换句话说，同一句“他是谁”，答案要看你问的是哪一支。", "src": "第 419、487、489、578、680 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 419 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 487 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 489 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 578 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 680 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-104-e0008", "zh": "你透过我的记忆而成长，就像一棵树透过空间成长一样，当你改变了，我的记忆也随着改变。我对你的记忆，包括你可能的自己，所有这些坐标同时存在于不占用空间的点上面。", "en": "You grow through my memory as a tree grows up through space, and my memory changes as you change. My memory of you includes your probable selves, and all these coordinates exist simultaneously in a point that takes up no space.", "source": "早期课第8册 · 第419节", "year": "1969", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第8册", "session": "419", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0010", "zh": "现在。自上次上课（6月9日）以来，曾有两度几近达成接触。（对方是我那身处另一个更为先进的实相中的可能的自己，以及皮特拉博士[Dr. Pietra]。）所发生的，是一次在深于意识的层面上极为短暂的融合。", "en": "Now. On two occasions since our last session (June 9), near contact was made. (With my probable self and Dr. Pietra in another more advanced reality.) What happened was a very momentary merging on deeper-than-conscious levels.", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第487节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "487", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0013", "zh": "你们同来自其他系统的旅客往来。你们在其他系统里种下思想形体，并且培护它们。你们与你们自己实相的众多部分聚会一堂。依我的体验，你们的世界就像是一个思想形体。你们的物理系统以种种强度存在，是可以被知觉到的。你们那些可能的自己，我和像我这样的存在都看得见，因为我们看见你们实相的一切维度；而你们呢，只从你们所认识的那些小小的当下形态去看它。（停顿。）", "en": "You visit with travelers from other systems. You plant thought forms in other systems, and you tend them. You hold congregation with the many segments of your own reality. Your world is like a thought form in my experience. Your physical system exists in many intensities, and can be perceived. Probable selves can be viewed by myself and others like me, for we see your reality in all of its dimensions, and you view it from the small present forms that you know. (Pause.)", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第489节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "489", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0036", "zh": "我对一个房间里的人们的感知与他们对自己的感知非常的不同；我可以知觉他们的各种前生与来世的人格，却无法知觉他们的可能自己。", "en": "I perceive people in a room in a far different manner than they perceive themselves; their various past and future reincarnated personalities, but not their probable selves, are perceivable to me.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第578节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "578", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0048", "zh": "显然，那创造性的、有机械发明才能的人格开始超过了另一个。所以，你所知的父亲是那可能的自己。不过，那可能的自己在处理另一个所避开的情感实相，而这的确是他唯一的意图。", "en": "By far, the creative, mechanically inventive personality began to outstrip the other. The father that you knew was the probable self, therefore. That probable self, however, dealt with emotional realities that the other avoided, and this was indeed his sole intent.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第680节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "680", "only_en": false}]}]}], "reading": [{"id": "q-concept-104-e0004", "zh": "再一次，目标应该是发展成为最好的可能的自己。现在与你亲密和年轻男人，在许多方面会有争执。", "en": "Again, the goal should be a development into the best possible self. There will be areas of contention with the young man with whom you are now involved.", "source": "早期课第7册 · 第329节", "year": "1968", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第7册", "session": "329", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0009", "zh": "不过你们必须留心找他。他知道你们这种假设性的存在。他相信他有这样一个可能的自己，并正竭力想要造访这个可能的宇宙。然而他一点也不知道，你们也许会被告知他的来访，或者你们也许正计划与他见面。他一直同另外两个人亲自在做那项药物的工作。", "en": "You must be looking for him however. He knows of your hypothetical existence. He believes he has such a probable self, and is endeavoring to visit this probable universe. He has no idea however that you might be told of his visits, or that you might be planning to meet him. He has been working on the drug himself along with two others.", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第486节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "486", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0014", "zh": "鲁柏也许能够。然而，强烈的推动力在你们可能的自己以及你们这一方。鲁柏更习惯于从梦境状态中工作，他有可能建立连接。不过在那条可能的实相当中，你们那位医生朋友并没有遇到一个可能的珍。", "en": "Ruburt may be able to. The strong impetus however is on the part of your probable self and yourself. Ruburt is more used to working from the dream state and possibly could make connection. In that probable reality however your doctor friend did not meet a probable Jane.", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第492节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "492", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0016", "zh": "然而，他们一定会去他们的经验能被最好利用的地方，去他们对毁灭的切身认识能被最好地用作工具的地方。在某种程度上，这样一个毁灭在所有实相里都会被感觉到。一些可能的自己会做粉碎性的灾难之梦。别的世界会因心灵的反映而震动。", "en": "They would however definitely go where their experience could be best utilized, and where their intimate knowledge of destruction be best used as a tool. To some degree such a destruction would be felt in all realities. Some probable selves would have shattering dreams of disaster. Other worlds would quake with the psychic reflections.", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第499节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "499", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0023", "zh": "那么，每个可能的实相系统自然也就创造了其他的这种系统，而任何一个已实现的行为，在其背后，都会带来无限量的“没实现”的行为，而这些没实现的行为终究也都会实现。且说所有的实相系统都是开放的，它们之间的分界是为了方便而武断地决定的，但所有实相全都同时存在，每一个都彼此支持并彼此增益。因此你的所作所为也都反映在你“可能的自己”的经验之上，反之亦然。", "en": "Each probable system of reality of course then creates other such systems, and any one act, realized, brings forth an infinite number of “unrealized” acts that will also find their actualization. Now all systems of reality are open. The divisions between them are arbitrarily decided upon as a matter of convenience, but all exist simultaneously, and each one supports and adds to the other. So what you do is also reflected to some degree in the experience of your probable selves, and vice versa.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第565节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "565", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0035", "zh": "那么，这类“存有”的意识也包括了很多的可能的自己与可能的系统的意识，而这些可能的自己与可能的系统都十分生动、清楚的被体验为“多重的现在”。这些“多重现在”能在无限个点的任一点被改变；所谓“无限”并非以一条无限的线那样存在着，而是指由意识的每一个生出来的无量的可能性及“可能的组合”。", "en": "Now the consciousness of such beings would also contain the consciousness of large numbers of probable selves and systems, experienced quite vividly and clearly as multiple presents. These multiple presents can be altered at any of an actual number of infinite points; infinity not existing in terms of one indefinite line, but in terms of numberless probabilities and possible combinations growing out of each act of consciousness.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第567节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "567", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0039", "zh": "有这种扩展能力的你，必然是个远比你先前想象的自己更有创造力与更具多次元的人格。我先前在这书里所建议的许多小练习，也会助你认识你自己的实相，会让你直接体验你自己的灵魂或存有的本质，并将使你与“你自己活力所源出的你存在的那些部分”接触。你也许会，也许不会与你过去的转世的自己或可能的自己碰面。你也许会也许不会当场捉住你自己正在改变你意识的层面。", "en": "The “you” who is capable of such expansion must be a far more creative and multidimensional personality than you earlier imagined. Many of the suggested small exercises given earlier in the book will also help you become acquainted with your own reality, will give you direct experience with the nature of your own soul or entity, and will put you in contact with those portions of your being from which your own vitality springs. You may or may not have your own encounters with past reincarnational selves or probable selves. You may or may not catch yourselves in the act of changing levels of consciousness.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第591节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "591", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-104-e0050", "zh": "就像细胞长成器官一样，意识的组织也会“长”。那么，一群可能自己可以，并且的确会形成它们自己的本体结构，而这个结构对所参与的可能自己是颇为觉察的。在你们的实相里，经验是依赖时间的，但并非所有的经验都是如此被结构的，举例来说，有些平行的时间也被很容易的跟随，就像你跟随有顺序的事件一样。", "en": "(A one-minute pause at 9:50, head bowed, eyes closed.) The organizations of consciousness “grow” even as cells grow into organs. Groups of probable selves, then, can and do form their own identity structure, which is quite aware of the probable selves involved. In your reality, experience is dependent upon time, but all experience is not so structured. There are, for example, parallel events that are followed as easily as you follow consecutive events.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第681节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "681", "only_en": true}], "books": [{"name": "灵魂永生", "count": 22}, {"name": "未知的实相卷一", "count": 12}, {"name": "早期课第9册", "count": 9}, {"name": "未知的实相卷二", "count": 7}, {"name": "早期课第7册", "count": 4}, {"name": "个人实相的本质", "count": 4}, {"name": "早期课第8册", "count": 4}, {"name": "个人与群体事件的本质", "count": 1}, {"name": "梦、进化与价值完成", "count": 1}], "span": [1968, 1979], "stats": {"blocks": 10, "quotes": 64, "reading": 8, "reading_tagged": 0, "tagged": 0, "only_en": 4}};
